capability url/parse  a URL read into its parts: scheme, user and password, host, port, path, query, fragment
  heroes  url.parse
  go      url.Parse, url.URL, url.URL.Scheme, url.URL.Opaque, url.URL.Host, url.URL.Path, url.URL.RawPath, url.URL.RawQuery, url.URL.Fragment, url.URL.RawFragment, url.URL.UnmarshalBinary
  rust    -
  python  urlsplit, SplitResult, SplitResult.scheme, SplitResult.netloc, SplitResult.path, SplitResult.query, SplitResult.fragment, splittype, splitquery, splittag, scheme_chars
  note    read by RFC 3986, strictly: a space, a `[` in a query, a character above ASCII and a `%` that begins no escape are `invalid`, where Go refuses only the last, and not in a query, and Python none. A relative path whose first segment holds a `:` ("192.168.0.1:80/x") is `invalid` as in Go and by RFC 3986 § 4.2, where Python reads a path. "mailto:a@b" has the path "a@b", the RFC's and Python's reading, where Go keeps it in `Opaque`. The path, the query and the fragment are kept raw (Python's; Go decodes the path and the fragment and keeps a raw hint), the user, the password and the host decoded (Go's). Rust's standard library has nothing in this area

capability url/parse-loose  a URL read from text a person typed or a server sent, what a URL may not hold written as its escape
  heroes  url.parse_loose
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    Go's `url.Parse` and Python's `urlsplit` are this reading by default, and have no strict one; here the strict reading is `parse` and this one is asked for by name. "http://h/a b", "http://h/café", "http://h/a\"b" and "http://h/a^b" are written back as Go's `String` writes them

capability url/parse-request-target  the target of an HTTP request read: an absolute path with its query, or an absolute URL
  heroes  url.parse_request_target
  go      url.ParseRequestURI
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    RFC 9112 § 3.2's origin-form and absolute-form; a `#` is `invalid`, where Go takes it into the path ("/p#f" is the path "/p#f"), and "*" is no URL, where Go answers the path "*". A target that begins with "//" is a path, as in Go

capability url/empty-parts  a part that is there and empty told from one that is absent
  heroes  url.parse, url.format
  go      url.URL.ForceQuery, url.URL.OmitHost
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    RFC 3986 § 6.2.3: a delimiter is not removed where its part is empty. Go keeps "?", "user:@" and "@" and drops "#"; Python keeps all of them since 3.15 where `missing_as_none` is asked. Here all are kept, by the five `empty_` flags of `url.Url`. An empty port ("h:") is read as none, RFC 3986 § 3.2.3, where Go and Python keep the ":"

capability url/params  the parameters after a `;` in the last segment of a path
  heroes  refused: RFC 3986 § 3.3 made the `;` of a segment each scheme's business, no longer the generic syntax's; `url.parse_path` answers a segment with its `;`, and `strings` cuts it
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  urlparse, urlunparse, ParseResult, ParseResult.params, uses_params, splitattr
  note    Python reads RFC 1808's parameters of the last segment alone ("/a;x/b;y" has the parameters "y"); Go has none

capability url/format  the text of a URL built back from its parts
  heroes  url.format
  go      url.URL.String, url.URL.EscapedPath, url.URL.EscapedFragment, url.URL.MarshalBinary, url.URL.AppendBinary
  rust    -
  python  urlunsplit, SplitResult.geturl
  note    RFC 3986 § 5.3. What a part may not hold is written as its escape, as Go does, so the answer is a URL; Python writes the parts as they are. A scheme or a port no escape can write aborts, a precondition the caller can check and `parse` never breaks. A first segment holding a ":" with neither scheme nor authority gets "./" before it, as in Go

capability url/redacted  the text of a URL with its password hidden, for a log
  heroes  url.redacted
  go      url.URL.Redacted
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    "xxxxx" in place of the password, Go's, an empty password that is written included

capability url/request-target  the path and the query as an HTTP request writes them
  heroes  url.request_target
  go      url.URL.RequestURI
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    an empty path is "/", Go's answer; a path that begins with no "/" gets one

capability url/is-absolute  whether a URL has a scheme
  heroes  url.is_abs
  go      url.URL.IsAbs
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    a scheme makes it absolute, Go's reading and RFC 3986 § 4.3's

capability url/resolve  a reference resolved against a base
  heroes  url.resolve
  go      url.URL.ResolveReference, url.URL.Parse
  rust    -
  python  urljoin, uses_relative, uses_netloc
  note    RFC 3986 § 5.2, every example of § 5.4 tested. The strict reading: "http:g" against "http://a/b/c/d;p?q" is "http:g", Go's answer, where Python answers "http://a/b/c/g"; the two agree with the RFC on the 41 others. Every scheme is resolved, where Python resolves only the ones in `uses_relative` ("d" against "foo://a/b/c" is "d" there). Go's `URL.Parse` is `url.parse` then `url.resolve`

capability url/remove-fragment  a URL without its fragment
  heroes  url.without_fragment
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  urldefrag, DefragResult, DefragResult.url, DefragResult.fragment, DefragResult.geturl
  note    Python answers the text and the fragment apart; here the fragment stays readable in the `url.Url` it came from

capability url/userinfo  the user and the password of a URL, read and written
  heroes  url.parse, url.format
  go      url.URL.User, url.User, url.UserPassword, url.Userinfo, url.Userinfo.Username, url.Userinfo.Password, url.Userinfo.String
  rust    -
  python  SplitResult.username, SplitResult.password, splituser, splitpasswd
  note    the fields `user` and `password` of `url.Url`, decoded, Go's reading, where Python answers them raw; a ":" in either is written "%3A", as Go writes it

capability url/host  the host alone, an IPv6 address without its brackets
  heroes  url.parse
  go      url.URL.Hostname
  rust    -
  python  SplitResult.hostname, splithost
  note    the field `host` of `url.Url`, its ASCII letters in lower case, Python's and RFC 3986 § 6.2.2.1's, where Go keeps the case. In a host an escape writes a character above ASCII or a letter, a digit, `-`, `.`, `_`, `~`, and any other is `invalid`: Go refuses every ASCII escape, Python decodes none. "[fe80::1%25en0]", a zone, is `invalid`: RFC 3986 has none and RFC 9844 obsoleted RFC 6874, which Go and Python read. "[v1.a]", an address of a future version, is `invalid` as in Go, where Python reads it: no version beyond 6 is in use, and its host could not be told from a name once its brackets are gone. A host above ASCII is kept as written, and `url/idna` writes it in ASCII by UTS #46 (`idna.to_ascii`) and back (`idna.to_unicode`), a package apart so that only its users carry its tables (about 289 KB, measured on 2026-11-17 as a program converting a host against one reading `unicode` alone)

capability url/port  the port a URL is reached on, as a number
  heroes  url.port_of
  go      url.URL.Port
  rust    -
  python  SplitResult.port
  note    a number, read through `numbers`; past 65535 is `does_not_fit`, where Python raises and Go's `Port` answers the text; with no port written, the scheme's default, where both answer none

capability url/host-port  a host and a port told apart in "host:port", and joined into it, an IPv6 host in brackets
  heroes  url.parse_host_port, url.format_host_port
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  splitport, splitnport
  note    Go's are `net.SplitHostPort` and `net.JoinHostPort`, outside this area

capability url/default-port  the port of a scheme where a URL writes none
  heroes  url.default_port
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    neither library offers one in this area. The table holds 32 schemes: those this machine's `/etc/services` names with the scheme's own name, read with Python's `socket.getservbyname` on 2026-11-15, and `ws`, `wss`, `sips`, `coap`, `coaps` from their RFCs. `sftp` and `news` are left out: the services table gives their names to other protocols (115, 2009)

capability url/path-segments  the segments of a path, unescaped, read and written
  heroes  url.parse_path, url.format_path
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    Go answers the whole path decoded in `URL.Path`, where "%2F" and "/" can no longer be told apart; here the path stays raw and its segments are read one by one

capability url/join-path  a URL whose path goes on with more segments
  heroes  url.add_segments
  go      url.JoinPath, url.URL.JoinPath
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    each segment is escaped, where Go takes them escaped already, and "." and ".." are not removed: that is `url.remove_dot_segments`, asked for apart. "http://h/v1" with "users" and "7", "http://h/v1/" with "a b" and "http://h" with "a" answer what Go answers

capability url/clean-path  a path without its "." and ".." segments
  heroes  url.remove_dot_segments
  go      url.JoinPath, url.URL.JoinPath
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    RFC 3986 § 5.2.4; Go does it inside `JoinPath` and also merges runs of "/", which the RFC does not

capability url/escape-segment  a text written as one segment of a path
  heroes  url.escape_segment
  go      url.PathEscape
  rust    -
  python  quote
  note    every character but a letter, a digit, `-`, `.`, `_`, `~` is escaped: RFC 3986 § 2.2 asks it of data, and it is Python's `quote(s, safe="")`. Go leaves `$&+:=@` as they are

capability url/escape-query  a text written as a key or a value of a query
  heroes  url.escape_query
  go      url.QueryEscape
  rust    -
  python  quote_plus
  note    the two agree on every printable ASCII character, and so does this

capability url/escape-fragment  a text written as a fragment
  heroes  url.escape_fragment
  go      url.URL.EscapedFragment
  rust    -
  python  quote
  note    `/` and `?` stay, which RFC 3986 § 3.5 allows as data in a fragment, and every other reserved character is escaped: Python's `quote(s, safe="/?")`. Go leaves `!$&()*+,:;=@` too

capability url/escape-bytes  bytes that are no text written as escapes
  heroes  url.escape_bytes
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  quote_from_bytes
  note    Python's `quote_from_bytes(b, safe="")`

capability url/unescape  the text an escaped segment, fragment or name stands for
  heroes  url.unescape
  go      url.PathUnescape
  rust    -
  python  unquote
  note    a `%` that begins no escape is `invalid`, Go's answer, where Python leaves it; bytes that are not UTF-8 are `not_text`, where Go answers the bytes and Python U+FFFD

capability url/unescape-query  the text an escaped key or value of a query stands for, a `+` read as a space
  heroes  url.unescape_query
  go      url.QueryUnescape
  rust    -
  python  unquote_plus
  note    as `url/unescape`

capability url/unescape-bytes  the bytes escapes stand for, text or not
  heroes  url.unescape_bytes
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  unquote_to_bytes
  note    a `%` that begins no escape is `invalid`, where Python leaves it

capability url/query-parse  the keys and values of a query, in their order, a key held more than once kept
  heroes  url.parse_query
  go      url.ParseQuery, url.URL.Query, url.Values
  rust    -
  python  parse_qsl, parse_qs, splitvalue
  note    a list of `url.Pair`, Python's `parse_qsl`, where Go's map loses the order of the keys. "a&b=1" holds the key "a" with an empty value, Go's answer and Python's with `keep_blank_values`. A `;` is a character of its value, Python's reading and the form's own rule, where Go fails. "%zz" is `invalid`, Go's answer, where Python keeps it; "%FF" is `not_text`, where Go answers the byte and Python U+FFFD

capability url/query-format  a query written from keys and values
  heroes  url.format_query
  go      url.Values.Encode
  rust    -
  python  urlencode
  note    in the order given, Python's, where Go sorts by key

